>- la rik. lu mi cliva li'u
>- cu'u la rik. lu mi cliva li'u
>
>Aren't some of these expressions ungrammatical? Still, they are being used.

I don't think any are ungrammatical.  They have different denotations
but connote the same thing.

(Rick says:) I leave.
Rick says, "I leave".
Rick, "I leave".
Said by Rick, "I leave"

In the latter two, the relation between the two sumti is unspecified and
therefore could be something other than the obvious in some contexts.
The first two correspond to two different styles of reporting conversation
in English - the first is used in printed drama, and the second is used
in narrative that includes reported statements.  The latter two occur in
colloquial English with particles that mark the colloquial - my daughter
might say "And Rick's, like, "I'm leaving".  And Jane's, like "Go ahead".

